{"title":"Heat equation in three dimensions","markdown":{"headingText":"Heat equation in three dimensions","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-heat3d","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n`apps/heat3d` — the reference application for comparing discretisations,\nbecause it ships several of them for the *same* PDE.\n\n## Physical setting\n\nLinear diffusion. The physics is not the point: this application exists so that\nfinite-difference order, spectral treatment, timing, and scaling can be\ncompared without the comparison being confounded by a difference in the\nproblem.\n\n## Governing equations\n\n$$\n\\partial_t u \\;=\\; D\\,\\nabla^2 u\n$$ {#eq-h3-pde}\n\non a periodic box, with a single fixed $D$ shared by every binary so their\nerrors against the analytic solution are directly comparable.\n\nFor a single Fourier mode the exact solution is\n\n$$\nu_{\\mathbf{k}}(t) \\;=\\; u_{\\mathbf{k}}(0)\\,e^{-D|\\mathbf{k}|^2 t},\n$$ {#eq-h3-exact}\n\nwhich every driver is measured against.\n\n## Finite-difference formulation\n\nCentred stencils of even order $2m$ approximate the Laplacian,\n\n$$\n\\partial_{xx}u\\big|_i \\;\\approx\\; \\frac{1}{\\Delta x^2}\\sum_{j=-m}^{m} a_j\\,u_{i+j},\n$$ {#eq-h3-stencil}\n\nwith $m$ ghost cells exchanged per face before each evaluation. Orders 2\nthrough 20 are available. Time stepping is explicit, subject to @eq-cfl.\n\n## Spectral formulation\n\nThe spectral drivers use the same reciprocal-space convention as the rest of\nthe report. With $u = k_{\\mathrm{lap}}$ the operator is simply $L(k) = D\\,u$,\nand the implicit Euler driver applies\n\n$$\n\\hat u^{\\,n+1} \\;=\\; \\frac{\\hat u^{\\,n}}{1 - \\Delta t\\,D\\,u},\n$$ {#eq-h3-implicit}\n\nwhich is two transforms per step and unconditionally stable.\n\n## The five drivers\n\n| Binary | Discretisation | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `heat3d_fd_scratch` | hand-written stencil | teaching: no framework abstractions |\n| `heat3d_fd_manual` | fixed second order | manual halo management |\n| `heat3d_fd` | orders 2–20 | convergence and order studies |\n| `heat3d_spectral` | pointwise RHS | spectral evaluation of the RHS |\n| `heat3d_spectral_pointwise` | implicit Euler, @eq-h3-implicit | unconditionally stable reference |\n\nGPU twins `heat3d_fd_hip` and `heat3d_spectral_hip` mirror the FD and spectral\npaths on device. **These two are what make @sec-scalability a method\ncomparison rather than an application comparison** — same equation, same\ngrids, same machine, two discretisations.\n\n## Solution strategy\n\nExplicit stepping for the FD drivers under @eq-cfl; implicit Euler in Fourier\nspace for the spectral drivers, at two transforms per step.\n\nThe trade is the usual one. The FD step is cheap and local — a stencil plus a\nhalo exchange with nearest neighbours — but the stable timestep shrinks as\n$\\Delta x^2$. The spectral step is unconditionally stable but costs two\nall-to-all transforms, which is global communication. Which wins depends on the\naccuracy required and on how well the machine's network carries an FFT\ntranspose; @sec-scalability measures exactly that.\n\n## Verification\n\n* **Analytic $L^2$ error.** Every driver is compared against @eq-h3-exact.\n* **Observed order.** Measured convergence matches the design order of the\n  stencil.\n* **CPU/GPU agreement.** `HEAT3D_HIP_CHECKSUM` agrees between 1 and 16 GCDs to\n  about $5\\times10^{-15}$ relative; the spectral twin agrees to about\n  $3\\times10^{-16}$.\n\n## Scalability\n\nMeasured on LUMI-G for both HIP twins, 1–32 GCDs. See @sec-scalability.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart